#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077} :activity/id #uuid "da7c19c9-a092-5227-8c3d-c60c670a8222" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199560 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $A$ and $B$ are sets, the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs $(a,b)$ whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(a, b) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B \\big\\}\n$$\n\nMany Cartesian products have natural representations in the Cartesian plane. The most common of these is the Cartesian product $\\mathbb R^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^2 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y) \\: : \\: x,y \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis infinite set contains all ordered pairs $(x,y),$ where $x$ and $y$ can be any real numbers. In other words, $\\mathbb R^2$ contains every point in the Cartesian plane! \n\n![](images/07/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)\n\nSimilarly, the set\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^3 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y, z) \\: : \\: x,y,z \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\ndescribes every point in three-dimensional Cartesian space. \n\n![](images/e7/e74fb5948144bc61173e4de73865cb746278bd31fd87e4abf927fa01821ba936.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c18e6618-573b-5560-8d95-58ed56f05155" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8237 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c7652427-e5ff-5d02-9f23-39039f090aff" :step/math-academy-id 153383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198169 :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422996}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "2190f1df-5853-5d8c-85e2-33f40f1f0f24" :step/math-academy-id 153384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199579 :tutorial/content "Cartesian coordinates can also be used to visualize other Cartesian products.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following Cartesian product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^- = \\big\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in\\mathbb N,\\, y\\in\\mathbb R^-\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis product contains every point in $\\mathbb R^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative real number.\n\nTo visualize this Cartesian product, consider the following subset of our Cartesian product:\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\big\\{ (1,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. We can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f6165f66b4149b7b2ea52a3cd686836853d85048ca890e850cb771174a150c57.png)\n\nNext, we consider the subset $S_2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ (2,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-.\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. \n\n![](images/ca/caf2299a2e8009ad5b2776ce67786fa7c7e3bdb57aacbd7df87882f59a2350e2.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3, S_4, S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N\\times {R}^{-} & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-$ as the set of vertical rays shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/033c40b86bfd5cebf24d34de644f632213207386c2b67c559af7594468599e1e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1626e562-8e88-5ff3-bae3-4162438c0086" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8329 :tutorial/title "Cartesian Products of Countably Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "cd371945-c79b-5b78-9017-15c39e4d5ed7" :step/math-academy-id 153387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198154 :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422995}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "48b1fb46-7071-5897-9dd7-a47e1d955f6c" :step/math-academy-id 153388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199584 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch a representation of the set $S= [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane.\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [0, 1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in [0,1],\\, y\\in [0,1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_0 \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}0}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{red}0} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{red}{0}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_{\\color{red}0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general $S_{\\color{blue}{i}} \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{blue}{i}} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{i}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor each ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1],$ $S_{\\color{blue}{i}}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},0)$ to the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},1).$\n\n![](images/28/28b9aa0559635ed4090f10dd0b59f2cbb6c297f8736d9509a637b5d78d27561c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I = [0,1]{:}$\n\n$$\nS = \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}.\n$$\n\nAs $\\color{blue}i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}{:}$\n\n![](images/76/76ff48755dbfc3ba1943665d08aa8cfd00948bbc1ddfaab560a0af64f11bae26.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "923fb377-d694-5181-8561-c57612f15054" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8339 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Product [0,1] x [0,1]"} :step/id #uuid "ef01039c-9cce-558a-8067-2ffba087e848" :step/math-academy-id 153392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198205 :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390333}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "9ddfd97d-7a1a-59ef-8492-3fbe4088f22f" :step/math-academy-id 153393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198206 :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427501}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5777ec1b-f050-570f-86e4-1e7bffa77dfb" :step/math-academy-id 153396}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198532}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427503} :activity/id #uuid "0974eb57-6659-54d9-be86-ddfa7868dbe6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13902432-1e15-5d83-9e31-77ea1c9ecb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9a/9a87c5ea8ceaa8a64e30b6375a8bcaa58182ac2e97d68b44c70a0a964c4727dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284963} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc8b5e0-b1db-5812-b923-c8b2aed030ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/34/340f534cb7548f2e5fcffb6153d45fd327b414aa17247bcde00b5396d24830a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285222} {:answer/id #uuid "95737d26-61bc-5532-92fe-b8144479975c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/32/3274c0747b72b6b8ce457f20cd89d0f7d951c8a3bd138c9061448c1bedb42b55.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286146} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d0322b-eee5-55b7-919c-66200da95a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/82/824d176d4d041be340eb786ff53f8b5a352edca3ad928fc593b9799f2cc4008b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286456} {:answer/id #uuid "ed33c550-8d8c-5c22-8aed-eb90798b7c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b6/b676b2201a4fe4d57cb005eb9527f429068eae4d18c55496ebabf9390fc19219.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284963} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc5ac333-7088-55b5-8dff-47ad3124a244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8eb13b5c-5049-574a-9dd7-f6e99016a3c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-182001" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times [-1,0]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times [-1,0]\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a integer number and whose $y$-coordinate belongs to $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in [-1,0]\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,0]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/63/63199d9f35c331339e97ad3b2094a520d1fb6a9892bd0beac83538c43b9e2748.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/03/03a6a90b3eff572ceed341de55a0d5e625e338ca18f2ae8ee85eed3bb5278167.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times [-1,0]$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a87c5ea8ceaa8a64e30b6375a8bcaa58182ac2e97d68b44c70a0a964c4727dd.png)"} :step/id #uuid "2f868252-f8e0-5a5f-92f0-b020aafff313" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c00cf5df-4240-5b04-850f-1b4b0d6f8ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c8/c87871c50b343792c73fc18dfe92bc2065b18ed3de1e7bb70324b8b43d027206.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389799} {:answer/id #uuid "1f633a02-8088-56db-88c2-7889ba264321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/39/39db7bc1b259b222a2365441babc3421101961ea26a7143af14b526ba7f81200.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389800} {:answer/id #uuid "6c189e68-bec8-518b-a64d-56fe2a4c4033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ec/ec0e1df36bf5d93b8cdbcc3d14cd510fbe299b929ed3d19f4d901f4461093305.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389801} {:answer/id #uuid "f10813b1-b163-59a5-a38f-0b5b4e7dded5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f9/f90de7c6d88b187af3604912a647620fb88d4833e08fd086cf1a9daae52f331f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389802} {:answer/id #uuid "81b3b685-552f-53ef-8556-c54cf7663b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/86/8627676ae3b7bcdeff423d79d7289d1d3f9243e0cc69d4f3ac7d3e5c51141fc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389800} :answer-field/id #uuid "f376ba00-5dab-590e-a791-4441f99a66cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60baa69f-bbf2-5905-831d-ab5e879fa2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-177648" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,1].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times [0,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,0.5],b\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0.5,0),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5b/5b882f3fb0e7b44eb2df3a510260574e5c4a4d85842a9c0d65515f1a40894756.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,i],b\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(i,0),$ $(i,1),$ and $(0,1).$\n\n![](images/cb/cb530698591dbd71a8058d9faa0c60b636dc654f21e92d65ec8295aeaa67355a.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [0,1]:$\n\n![](images/70/704f8f6f0666d09bb2b8a25eab0548c3eb808e2c9d394d2ae873de35cb0112d1.png)"} :step/id #uuid "49fb4645-c2a9-59e2-8599-72cac0d3d4bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1204bd88-b470-5b91-b7c2-a8d4ad537898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/46/463ae2387423a48dd557af8cfd9b01b6bc08b95f1b3c2f337c6b0c53d3731fa2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389918} {:answer/id #uuid "60caae02-3071-514b-9258-9f96b6664d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2d/2df87b90d770a5f2c0ba40a55330409d1cd80866b7e34299eab6dcbf675c6a13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389919} {:answer/id #uuid "746ae67c-caa3-593c-9e3f-51831b036f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f7/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389920} {:answer/id #uuid "c89a44bf-6d27-578e-9136-6babbb95e931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2f/2f28e6f8c8e6e9fa13b5285e25e5460d26548ffd9d71077d1c6073e6c4c743a1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389921} {:answer/id #uuid "51564c2b-bcad-5e6e-9c34-dbd7a16d3a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/58/5888cf137eea674995b4c9cc37285b4130df5de3a2a2fa63b1b5b195910526c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389920} :answer-field/id #uuid "c241a74d-c7f4-5b96-a451-f6a50f08f04a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6bae802a-d591-53eb-b628-21b012d03cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-181968" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={Z}^{-}\\times N$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e6816767cd2010fca1b8991ed9c5e0c42d3a6d6466d78025534bc8362a174b9.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/4d/4d0fe261e790520e6a8786e6f024d5b9cb7cd54c18242103a1f4b77f09653abc.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7ffe2ddd-605f-5cd8-bf11-9de23847ac5f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a224de-0a7a-5ebf-83b7-7575ba7f49c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f8/f8eb09869ae1733a58ed488cc747bca4dddf56ebc0da0de53ff21a2181208217.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284846} {:answer/id #uuid "72e2efff-69aa-52bb-a78c-e682719225d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/30/30c98beaaaf9728a31685195cd236464ceba34094aeda381838f33ba2a134b4c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285846} {:answer/id #uuid "ca095e46-57a5-5045-9532-8b19c4502e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/82/8210684df0b5143b3b51a3a30cd03e4cdbc4bb80d139c25385dd0fde668d6fd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286632} {:answer/id #uuid "d08d3be0-ab3c-598f-b81f-c0a937f9bb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ee/ee6ac65885afc561a40d51352bb6a4eac4974db27274cb35ad3032b7a7a8ffb4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286686} {:answer/id #uuid "efd1c7f0-4319-573c-83f6-fc4b435b4a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c2/c2474d727fe1d97a21f67e9c4ae71b19dcc98106bb7b34c3ccef19ae56b44487.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286632} :answer-field/id #uuid "39cb9311-07e3-5d93-89a4-7c78cbd6de5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10624e27-fb3c-5852-bfb2-5c112258247b" :question/math-academy-id "q-181984" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the set $S=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}=\\{(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,1],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](images/49/494c9d77b0ff339b90792270c9d641054a03a2918e3c56a93747b42a77ee3ccf.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,1]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/7c/7c5ac5dad0aaacb4347c260d634e6bcd2d6102b25d51813fe106803417cc7539.png)"} :step/id #uuid "8cd3cbad-1220-514c-ad2e-d949f26d97ee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b353f4d-b6c1-544c-a565-cbc81d0130e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/49/4928c291f6eb42646dd9623b7c665a1569462b1b5ce19b31501ae3c655303b87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427495} {:answer/id #uuid "89fe3c93-58d2-564c-8818-f614741ab1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ff/ffaa9807fa5155dcc848d68bed93a16b267045fae3f5d4d0945e064ad5c11eef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427496} {:answer/id #uuid "99bf3628-2a02-53d8-922c-2471074f7424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0a/0a655218c2ee2a93e2c37601b8d989f449395270fbea8ff2dda34caedccd95e1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427497} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8e4cd5-94f1-51c7-aff4-ee1d59197f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f1/f15dd410cc7b5e47cd246182606ef7e9fb5c51caae792815104ab788d0da9a48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427498} {:answer/id #uuid "d3489613-d07f-57e1-bd54-7d15cf117eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/bf/bf3963dc09b844714f7cbe85e93caf539b2de64cd6a5264d9a7477e724ea5a8b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427496} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e7343a-4633-545d-a3b9-3c2ada585e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46ba7fa0-d231-5401-8252-d4e156d84cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-184533" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times [-1,1].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-1,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(1,-1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/712970edb9966d6544cbe4a360aaa0de3436f356c4c7f23ff601c70ef168a521.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times [-1,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-i,i],b\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1),$ $(-i,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,-1).$\n\n![](images/68/68f8c51062bd9d3ecadd33b0a67ea0990f631375aba9f9cc705946c79abf47d0.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]:$\n\n![](images/ff/ffaa9807fa5155dcc848d68bed93a16b267045fae3f5d4d0945e064ad5c11eef.png)"} :step/id #uuid "c27fb01c-6abc-53a8-9ed3-81896b8737cf"}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427494}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494058} :activity/id #uuid "4ec125ac-e7e6-5672-8f33-3a74502489fe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63212b76-4dd2-5413-b5ba-4e9195ab76e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/82/82f9ecf98b8b2b4e5f75b3130e8483354c93922dc3a942e271562ac1ad17438e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389897} {:answer/id #uuid "4a858bfb-ee22-5b56-9050-fa66d28f0274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e0/e0fa3e717af02a353db77ae8b357dc338c975166fb5fbe6c5c1309d1c1f511f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389898} {:answer/id #uuid "7442f1d4-b621-53b9-a885-39076816c0b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c2/c28ba6b0da8364f4e7f157b58827384f9e138a2c0fc53558ce7ab2df2f646747.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389899} {:answer/id #uuid "281cc561-5401-5252-9068-734db096e5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/07/075454dae9ed36967a70537cdf3a3f59931cdd81671ff4059658d2e0df438898.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389900} {:answer/id #uuid "b42501d9-b115-5c79-8921-c1756b26a1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/29/2922ff5e3a32e01fbc292898c88c1d8eca8d933897d4b9d3c1694b6f5cbeb2a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389900} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f377c8c-6598-5676-856c-726dc4aca072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "734eb671-4f30-5ac2-952a-94e297467ed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-181306" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=[-1,1]\\times \\{-1,1\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times \\{-1,1\\}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is either $-1$ or $1.$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,y\\in \\{-1,1\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3ed05ffc58aad1a6c75e7862c1175f497fb14870e6650cc6c4ce01c3df091d8b.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0f57920b2532177b0d64ac3dc95feb6a476bb385112d736d702895862e7de2b2.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](images/07/075454dae9ed36967a70537cdf3a3f59931cdd81671ff4059658d2e0df438898.png)"} :step/id #uuid "273024b7-a3c5-551a-be72-b7286c27a144" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ad6e0a-c6f3-535a-b438-cd7ef33e552e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/47/47ee12b24246473a45e057d4da6dac5190a4eab23e18bb37bef494bf07627daa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284841} {:answer/id #uuid "67a76b1b-677d-5667-b8f6-e17c0a0eb5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/23/236d10f31265b400036e1ddeafa122991bcae1ea878af8b300003fb11acfa85b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285734} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb39817-73c5-556d-a591-eb6ab10a7c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ef/efb6d92ea0dc70fa0cbd9d82ea838b41a4eaab6ee2fc32c6fada9258a4154680.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285956} {:answer/id #uuid "f569f51b-ab90-59de-bd38-4bd9ff476d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/37/37de777f63941a62b003a4c600a40df648b3d39ce3c1dfc753c6f406fe230330.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287035} {:answer/id #uuid "fa732ef5-7dfb-5052-8ef8-ad4126c8c625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5e/5e474959f4172f2a3dc7d104a5b7e27a8eece4fc1f13421fefdfdfc2e139c139.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287074}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285734} :answer-field/id #uuid "053ceb7f-15f7-5209-b489-364d6abf0a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "673cc09b-aa8d-5b71-ab95-ac8a33662caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-184507" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2]\\times [-i,0].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,2]\\times [-0.5,0]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-0.5,0]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-0.5),$ and $(0,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/58/5837864bfcc40e680cf62b43c0e3d145ba05b1f25e1ccae2f4788902d030d927.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2]\\times [-i,0]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-i,0]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-i),$ and $(0,-i).$\n\n![](images/f0/f0c20436f03db4b0003d087827cefbd76506af423da3f0a3042082b5afeb4b58.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[0,2]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/23/236d10f31265b400036e1ddeafa122991bcae1ea878af8b300003fb11acfa85b.png)"} :step/id #uuid "631e5e2d-6b28-58a2-bd13-a4dacbfc7d52"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f001f66-21ad-5810-9a24-3727a01a58d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/95/95c7144f229cc34fcb98b1c1fa8bda8af8ca9dff68c7ae466e388ce3e525bc85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285523} {:answer/id #uuid "5829deab-1ed2-5ddf-959a-31d73972126d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ec/eceb9c773d52b8bae73837252a885258b5a9b735cf3f24954b8755049d7cf07d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285596} {:answer/id #uuid "64ba1242-9f38-5065-b2a2-156c1dffa463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285703} {:answer/id #uuid "6e22185e-2dca-5e48-a97a-c408b9904280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285800} {:answer/id #uuid "97b4fd05-3101-5cc2-857b-8c7b3c842a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/43/43f665cebf4c3af1f1178d94b4ceec5d60bcfd4e61ebf955ed43cb603f8232b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285800} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ea3661b-4d1c-56cf-956a-8769e8d2e497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5558c8b0-025f-54bf-b54a-ad58d686884f" :question/math-academy-id "q-181770" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3fdfed680e3fb46adbe8a70aac86682aacb85d43e0675923f87658635fc89a1e.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/7f/7fbda500ecca7e18c9db9939ad78d4dcc6bf488bc7fc4de1137e764ce21a0254.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times R$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7e3bf0ab-2167-59b1-a2c8-8f2c346337df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146d8845-efd5-5bc8-9da2-601d9879cc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/66/664f327f59e76cb22e1b9bd0a83f84c24e75ad6a0d176532437f629bf80bfc29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284969} {:answer/id #uuid "a2618d61-fba9-5cd1-88a1-8d9652f5d74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286277} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f95c53-3e3f-565a-9a4b-ea11c7c260f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4d/4ddec65745de14217112f6365d9dd372df5a24a13ef8bedc8db592c9ee18cab1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286281} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e480ca-6b20-5449-ba68-f8ceac3b823c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/05/05323cccad18bb4664fe74112ff312f53ef3e12016be6f7b30ca9146f2ba6119.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286290} {:answer/id #uuid "df934188-ff40-536d-b7a9-b50cdf29ebbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286277} :answer-field/id #uuid "0402905b-109e-5a9b-b5e8-b00b20ff4037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a66eead9-d6a1-5d8c-a676-bee7fbbe7fc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-181777" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times Z\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in Z\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e677c814756c4818c25da450d789c974787df60cf4bb18017a96593d3ecc180.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f9/f99beeb00c070048ee4ee7c1b6c9e077d7d4e0f3fd9e63aa12b5b58588e14dbb.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times Z & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times Z$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png)"} :step/id #uuid "b87742e1-5ccb-50d0-bb27-5711cd8741bd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b35462e3-d90d-5229-9bb7-a180bdbea833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/aa/aaa04c2578557d03dd144c27e6a5c223126830ba2a3f8288adc9239daf604e55.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389806} {:answer/id #uuid "32da30c7-4f73-5968-bd10-234d5aa6eb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/98/98077ef737a2487f9e480b7ffd42376785c5681e2651b98e7b71abb4e645d876.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389807} {:answer/id #uuid "94b8b364-2f47-562b-be4f-8d6d5b53993c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/36/36b36ac05cb1054e198f2e40a2d0ffdd40e952abaa8ddf5c946a38fde946adc8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389808} {:answer/id #uuid "1da4c351-345b-51fc-b9bc-106b693a793a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4a/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389809} {:answer/id #uuid "f684dbe0-2990-5f82-837b-23d70335f3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/74/749f4fb3a6416147cdd69c08bf806c28e2112929453bb8ae405c48452d82126b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389809} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a3b75ec-dbba-566e-adfa-fb7b15c6d5b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "62556693-fd48-5895-851c-d4aab5200ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-177650" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times \\{i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times \\{0.5\\}=\\{(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/75/7587daeaea45c5ef3449ecb676133c60bbedb7819308783bac835f3ae9c6883c.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times \\{i\\}=\\{(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/b9/b926204a3d58b25889f1c79dd0f65c1ea990c67bc88e869004396a77970e8dd4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/4a/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d95f77bb-e077-5521-bcdf-9c73957e19dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493986}}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493374}]}
